  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 1200 PM CDT Fri Sep 25 2026\n\nA scattered fair weather cu field has begun popping as of early\nthis afternoon, though conditions remain mostly sunny with\ntemperatures warming into the upper 60s to low 70s. Clearing skies\nand light winds may once again result in patchy fog after \nmidnight, though the setup remains conditional. Similar to this \nmorning, fog development will likely hinge on increasing cloud\ncover early Saturday morning, which may hinder radiational\ncooling. Will be worth monitoring given volatility over the past\ncouple of mornings. \n\nRain chances over the weekend continue to look rather pessimistic\nas high pressure wins the battle, as it so often does in this\nscenario. Models continue to show light rain splitting off to our\nnorth and south as low pressure ejects into northern Canada, \nleaving PoPs at a meager 15 to 20%. While a sprinkle or two \nwouldn't be out of the question over central or north-central \nWisconsin, expect most of the forecast area to remain dry.\n\nStarting to see similar decreasing trend with rain chances during\nthe middle of next week as abundant low-level dry air remains in\nplace beneath Hudson Bay high. Chances for widespread rainfall\nstill look on track as PWATs approach 90th percentile, though \nensembles begin to flatten out the upper trough as it approaches \nthe Midwest and encounters sturdy omega ridge. As such, probabilities\nfor one inch of rainfall have been reduced to around 20 to 30%.\nAdditionally, better moisture return and forcing look to remain\noff to our south, which would mean more of a glancing blow to our\nsouthern tier of counties. All in all, signs still point toward a\ndecent stratiform rain setup, though excessive rainfall potential\nremains in question due to prolonged duration and current drought\nstatus. Worth mentioning is that predictability remains fairly \nlow this far out in the forecast period, so there is still time \nfor things to change.",
